WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.293
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.293 --> 00:00:11.391
[FOREIGN] You are an 18 year old high
school

00:00:11.391 --> 00:00:16.640
exchange student in a small Bavarian town.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.650
You've been invited on a congressional
delegation to Berlin, Germany.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:26.160
And you are the youngest person there by
at least 30 years.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.080
You're wearing a business suit for

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.643
the first time ever you're feeling pretty
good.

00:00:33.643 --> 00:00:39.010
[LAUGH] And so you raise your hand and you
ask the Congressman.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.490
[FOREIGN].

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.230
What are your foreign policy goals?

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:49.625
And as the words confidently come out of
your mouth, you hear the audience gasp.

00:00:49.625 --> 00:00:53.630
[LAUGH]
Today,

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:58.900
I wanna speak to you about how language
shapes us and our relationships.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:01.380
In our globalized world,

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.320
people are to connect between far corners
of this planet.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.750
And as English becomes the [INAUDIBLE]
every day, more and

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.680
more of these conversations are happening
in a common language.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.940
But what happens when people begin
communicating

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.770
in a language that is not their own.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:21.600
A lot get's lost in translation.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.720
A lot more then just words.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.990
Our values, our beliefs and our norms are
embedded in our language.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:38.190
But it goes a step further, our language
influences how we see the world and

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:38.920
what we think about it.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.456
By not fully appreciating the importance
of language,

00:01:44.456 --> 00:01:50.240
are we short-changing relationships?

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.970
So as I crouched back in those
congressional chambers with 50 sets of

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:58.022
eyes staring at me, I wondered, what did I
do wrong?

00:01:58.022 --> 00:02:00.090
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.210
&gt;&gt; I replayed the words in my head.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:03.760
What are your foreign policy goals?

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.850
Couldn't figure it out.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.497
What are your for,

00:02:09.497 --> 00:02:15.125
[SOUND] [FOREIGN] I duted the congressman.

00:02:15.125 --> 00:02:19.460
I committed the cardinal sin of all German
language learners.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:24.390
You see in German, just as in French or
Spanish, there are two versions of

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:31.180
the second person pronoun, you, du and
Sie, the informal and the formal.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.220
I had learned this rule many times before,
but

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.065
I just glossed over those pages in my
textbooks.

00:02:35.065 --> 00:02:39.630
[LAUGH] I didn't understand why I should
have to address someone differently

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.195
just because they were older than me.

00:02:41.195 --> 00:02:46.360
[LAUGH] What I didn't realize was that in
ignoring this rule

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:50.220
I was ignoring an important social norm of
respect and hierarchy.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:56.690
As I addressed my congressman with the
informal due,

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:04.010
I offended him his office his reputation
and all 50 adults staring at me.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.700
By ignoring the importance of language,

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.530
I missed an opportunity to form a
meaningful relationship.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.060
Now, speaking of meaningful relationships,

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:20.052
how many of you remember the first time
you met your in-laws?

00:03:20.052 --> 00:03:22.490
Scary, right?

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:26.540
[LAUGH]
My fiance Alex

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.030
came with me to visit my extended family
in Belgrade,

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:33.080
Serbia, the city in which I was born,
about a year into our relationship.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:36.320
The day we landed I took him to my
favorite outdoor cafe and

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.280
as we sipped on an iced tea, he asked me.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.650
So what, who exactly are we meeting
tomorrow?

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.450
I started telling that to all my aunts and
uncles.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:51.740
I was so excited for it to him to meet but
he interrupted me,

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:55.315
how are your related to all of these
people?

00:03:55.315 --> 00:04:00.070
[LAUGH]
I grabbed a napkin and a pen and

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:03.538
I started drawing my family tree.' Well
you see,

00:04:03.538 --> 00:04:08.110
tomorrow: we're going to hang out with my
mom's side of the family.

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:09.830
I love her, aunt and uncle basically
raised her.

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:10.690
Slap that on the docket.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:11.820
And then her kids Sally and Sandra.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.410
And then their kids Mija, [UNKNOWN] and
Mijalid they're just like this to us.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:17.850
And the next day we're gonna hang out with
my dad's side of the family.'.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.430
Because my, my grandfather on my dad side,
he had four siblings.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.540
And all of them have obviously aunts,
there's aunts and uncles, and

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:23.930
then cousins and I then I guess grand
cousins,

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.170
I'm not really sure what you call it.

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.540
Many of them leave in Boston and Toronto,
but they're all here for the summer.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.190
[LAUGH] And then next week, we're gonna go
Eastern Serbia,

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:31.770
we're gonna have my mom's side of the
family.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:35.055
That grandfather had three siblings, so
there's a lot of them over there as well.

00:04:35.055 --> 00:04:41.240
[LAUGH]
So, as I rattled off my family tree.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.260
I realized how much was lost in
translation.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:49.380
In Serbian, I have words to describe if my
aunts and uncles are from my maternal or

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:50.810
paternal side.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.460
If they're related by blood or marriage.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:56.820
Chinese even has words to describe the
relative birth order

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:57.500
of your aunts and uncles.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:06.900
But in English, I only have two words aunt
and uncle.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.730
Embedded in our language are our values.

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:13.850
And if sharing and understanding one
another's values is one of the bedrocks

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:17.050
of, of meaningful relationships, are we
missing an opportunity?

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.730
Alex's immediately family means the world
to him.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:24.960
But I couldn't imagine a world without my
Serbian version

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.115
of My Big Fat Greek Wedding.

00:05:26.115 --> 00:05:31.370
[LAUGH] As I translated my family try to
him in English,

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:36.010
my value of extended family and community
was lost in translation.

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:43.410
Fully understanding our values is
critical.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:48.500
If we truly want to understand each other
are we missing an opportunity by not

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.320
fully understanding how language shapes
us?

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.830
Whether you're navigating you in-law's
family tree or

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:55.730
a multi-national corporation's org
structure,

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:00.170
you might be able to learn something about
the pe-, about what matters to the people

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:03.980
around you by understanding the words they
would use to describe their worlds.

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:06.850
Now I have a question for all of you.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.963
How would you describe the following
situation?

00:06:10.963 --> 00:06:11.514
Oh!

00:06:11.514 --> 00:06:12.614
Shit!

00:06:12.614 --> 00:06:18.934
[LAUGH]
Sorry about that.

00:06:18.934 --> 00:06:22.570
[LAUGH]
Now, imagine I broke the glass.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:25.850
How would describe what just happened?

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:33.040
English speakers, are more likely to say
Helena broke the glass.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:39.560
Where Spanish and Japanese speakers are
most likely to see the glass broke itself,

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:45.304
said [FOREIGN]
[LAUGH].

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:48.580
How do you think the way you answer that
question

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:52.560
impacts the way you see the world?

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:57.860
The cognitive scientist who ran the
initial glass experiment at Stanford found

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:01.760
out that when a glass was broken by
accident, English speakers were more

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:05.709
likely to remember who broke the glass
than Spanish or Japanese speakers.

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:11.250
They were more likely to assign agency to
an individual even in the event of

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:17.020
an accident, this has massive implications
on society.

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:20.720
Our language impacts how we see the world,
how we think about our relationships to

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:23.519
each other, to our government, how we
interpret financial crises.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:29.410
I understand that there's a chicken and
egg problem here.

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:32.660
Does language create culture or does
culture create language?

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:37.040
I am not gonna venture to answer that
question today,

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:38.705
I will leave that to the linguists.

00:07:38.705 --> 00:07:41.090
[LAUGH] The simple fact,

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:47.060
is that two people can interpret the exact
same event in two completely

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:51.160
different ways simply because of how they
formulate their sentences.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:57.400
By not fully appreciating how language
shapes our world view and

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.562
that of others, are we short-changing
relationships?

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:09.000
So when I think back to my 18 year old
self, confidently raising my hand and

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.920
anciently duding my congressman,

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:17.090
I realized how lucky I was to have
offended them and make that mistake.

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:23.624
Time and time again, I have seen how
language how our values, our norms,

00:08:23.624 --> 00:08:28.292
and our beliefs are embedded in and shaped
by our val, by our language.

00:08:28.292 --> 00:08:32.167
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:08:32.167 --> 00:08:35.730
Many of us will go on to live in a
globalized world.

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:36.550
But each and

00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:40.400
every one of us will continue to form
relationships across cultural ties.

00:08:41.540 --> 00:08:46.190
So I'd encourage you, just as I did, to
stop thinking about those rules

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:49.520
as being frustrating things you have to
learn in class, to learning more of them.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:54.190
I've gone from seeing language as a
barrier to building relationships,

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:56.040
to seeing it as an opportunity to deepen
them.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:02.420
So next time you are feeling pretty good.

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:04.635
Raising her hand, I'm ready to ask that
question.

00:09:04.635 --> 00:09:07.440
[LAUGH] Ask yourself first.

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:15.746
How does language shape me and how do I
wanna use it to shape my relationships?

00:09:15.746 --> 00:09:18.033
Thank you.

00:09:18.033 --> 00:09:19.417
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:19.417 --> 00:09:24.019
[MUSIC]

